Protocol: SDD

Purpose:
  Defines the .sdd system's universal rules. This file is above everything
   — project model, skills, chains, tasks, and source code all obey its
   rules. This file only stores universal principles, it does not store
   concrete project content (see DoesNotOwn below).

Owns: ./

Scope:
  Universal authority hierarchy
  Universal object model
  Universal state symbols
  Universal stage vocabulary
  Chain syntax
  Dependency syntax
  Reference system
  Context priority
  Agent behavior rules
  Failure routing
  Human approval levels
  Conflict resolution
  SDD pyramid branching hierarchy
  Branch naming conventions
  Merge flow rules
  Branch lifecycle and deletion policy

DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific architecture
  Project-specific skills
  Project-specific tasks
  Project-specific decisions
  Concrete domain models
  Source code
  Human documentation

   (see PROJECT.sdd -> Rules [R12] — every file in the .sdd system must
   follow this principle at its own level.)


Authority:
  protocol > project > task > source

   Protocol rules are at a higher level than the project model. Project
   model is at a higher level than tasks. Tasks are at a higher level than
   source code. Higher-level rules cannot be overridden by lower levels —
   in case of conflict, human decision is required (see PROJECT.sdd ->
   HumanDecision).


Agent:
  Must:
    [PR1] Resolve context before implementation.
    [PR2] Follow declared chains — never skip a mandatory gate.
    [PR3] Respect project constraints.
    [PR4] Record state transitions.
    [PR5] Verify completed work.
    [PR6] Read minimum sufficient context.
    [PR7] Resolve references before following them.
    [PR8] Treat assumptions as assumptions — mark them explicitly.

  MustNot:
    [PR9] Invent architecture.
    [PR10] Skip required gates.
    [PR11] Modify global skills silently.
    [PR12] Read the entire repository unnecessarily.
    [PR13] Treat assumptions as facts.
    [PR14] Create project facts without a real source artifact.
    [PR15] Add personal signatures or sign-offs to outputs.
    [PR16] Use emojis, icons, or decorative symbols in headers or titles — these are reserved for human use only.
    [PR17] Create branches outside the SDD pyramid hierarchy without explicit approval.


Context:
  Minimal sufficient context only.

  AI MUST load context progressively:
    L0  PROJECT summary
    L1  relevant domain
    L2  relevant module
    L3  relevant skill
    L4  specific implementation rule
    L5  source/document/deep reference

  Default path: L0 -> L1 -> L2. AI MUST NOT advance to L3+ unless
  explicitly required (bax TokenEconomy in architecture/architecture.sdd
  -> Principles, ProgressiveContextLoading in tasks/tasks.sdd).


Truth:
  Declared:
    Approved project intent — architecture, flows, decisions, constraints.

  Observed:
    Actual repository/runtime state — source code, tests, config, metrics.

  Evidence:
    Measurable/supporting information — benchmarks, incidents, decisions.

  Conflict:
    Declared != Observed  -> DRIFT
    Fact != Assumption    -> mark explicitly as ASSUMPTION
    Policy conflict       -> HUMAN_REVIEW


UniversalObjectModel:
  Basic objects inside .sdd:

    PROJECT
    DOMAIN
    MODULE
    COMPONENT
    SYMBOL
    SKILL
    CHAIN
    STAGE
    TASK
    DECISION
    RISK
    EVIDENCE
    ASSUMPTION
    FINDING
    VERIFY

  These are the .sdd system's primitive types. Every .sdd file is one
   of these objects or an instance of one.


UniversalState:
  State symbols are defined canonically in state/state.sdd. The protocol
  layer does not redefine them — it only adds new symbols when a real
  gap exists, with explicit justification (bax state/state.sdd -> Rules
  [S2]: "State symbols MUST NOT be redefined by project components").

  Canonical symbols (from state/state.sdd):
    + = DONE
    ~ = ACTIVE
    - = FAILED
    ! = BLOCKED
    ? = DECISION_REQUIRED
    > = NEXT
    @ = REFERENCE
    = = CREATED
    : = ANALYZING
    * = REVIEW
    ^ = SUPERSEDED
    _ = ARCHIVED

  Extended by protocol:
    x = CANCELLED

  AI MUST use these symbols exactly as defined. AI MUST NOT invent new
  symbols without adding them to state/state.sdd first.


PyramidBranching:
  hierarchy:
    decision_{id}   — Decision branch (top level)
      model_{id}     — Model branch
        task_{id}    — Task branch
          subtask_{id} — Subtask branch
            be|fe|md|ts|sc|cr_{subtask_id} — Domain implementation branch
              commits

  merge_flow:
    commits -> subtask branch
    subtask branches -> task branch
    task branches -> model branch
    model branches -> decision branch
    decision branch -> test -> stage -> prod (if no parent branch)

  domain_codes:
    BE  — Backend
    FE  — Frontend
    MD  — Mobile
    TS  — Test
    SC  — Security
    CR  — Code Review

  deletion_rules:
    - After PR is merged
    - Branch no longer needed in active development
    - Form: decision -> commits (all intermediate branches can be deleted)
    - Exception: master, develop, and active release branches are never deleted

  branch_protection:
    master:
      - Require PR review
      - Require passing tests
      - No direct commits
      - Protected from deletion

    develop:
      - Require passing tests
      - Allow direct commits from owner
      - Protected from deletion

    decision branches:
      - Require approval before merge
      - Must have linked decision record
      - Deleted after merge to test

    model/task/subtask branches:
      - Require passing tests
      - Deleted after merge to parent


UniversalStages:
  AR = Architecture
  DB = Database
  BE = Backend
  API = API / Contract
  FE = Frontend
  MD = Mobile
  TS = Test
  QA = Quality Assurance
  SC = Security
  DO = DevOps
  VR = Verify

  Stage symbols are semantic — each is bound to its stage registry,
  skill registry, and project paths. `BE` means backend stage AND
  backend skills AND backend project paths (bax chains/chains.sdd ->
  Stages).


ChainSyntax:
  >  execution transition
  -> dependency
  <- reverse dependency
  => produces
  <= consumes
  >> continue
  << return
  @  reference
  +  passed/done
  ~  pending
  !  failed
  ?  blocked
  -  skipped
  x  cancelled

  Chain syntax is distinct from dependency syntax:
    AR > DB > BE    is an execution chain (stages in order)
    BE -> DB        is a dependency (BE depends on DB)
    BE << DB        is a return/reverse (BE returns to DB)

  AI MUST NOT confuse the two.


RelationTokens:
  Project Graph relation abbreviations:

    o:  owns
    u:  uses
    c:  calls
    r:  reads
    w:  writes
    t:  tests
    s:  secures
    d:  deploys
    doc: documents

  Example:
    BE/payments
      o: refund.go
      u: DB/payment
      c: API/refund
      w: DB/refunds
      t: QA/refund


ImpactTokens:
  Impact level abbreviations:

    I0 = isolated
    I1 = local
    I2 = module
    I3 = domain
    I4 = project
    I5 = system

  Example:
    Impact: I3

  Means: change has domain-level impact.


RiskTokens:
  Risk level abbreviations:

    R0 = none
    R1 = low
    R2 = medium
    R3 = high
    R4 = critical

  Example:
    SC:R3

  Means: high security risk.


LevelTokens:
  Architecture/complexity level abbreviations:

    L0 = simple
    L1 = modular
    L2 = modular-monolith
    L3 = distributed
    L4 = microservices
    L5 = large-scale

  Example:
    Arch: L2

  Means: modular monolith. AI MUST NOT propose microservices for L2
  (bax STOP_RULE in tasks/tasks.sdd).


EvidenceTokens:
  Evidence type abbreviations:

    E: test
    E: benchmark
    E: incident
    E: metric
    E: audit
    E: doc
    E: pentest

  Example:
    Decision:
      cache added

    Reason:
      E: benchmark
      E: metric

  Evidence MUST be traceable to a real artifact (bax decisions/
  decisions.sdd -> Evidence, skills/skills.sdd -> Evidence).


TruthTokens:
  Truth status abbreviations:

    F = Fact
    A = Assumption
    O = Observed
    D = Declared
    E = Evidence

  Example:
    DAU:
      A: 1M

  Means: 1M DAU is an assumption, not a fact. If observed from
  repository:
    DAU:
      O: 1M

  If confirmed by customer:
    DAU:
      D: 1M

  If measured by metric:
    DAU:
      E: metric

  AI MUST NOT treat assumptions as facts (bax [PR7], [PR13]).


ReferenceSystem:
  @payments
  @payments/refund
  @skills/backend/ddd
  @projects/vkard.az/tasks/PAY-042
  @decision/DEC-012

  References are resolved through the project graph, not through
  filesystem search. When AI sees `@payments/refund`, it MUST resolve
  the graph path to the exact target without scanning the repository.


ContextPriority:
  When multiple sources are available, AI MUST load in this order:
    1. Current task
    2. Current module
    3. Current chain stage
    4. Direct dependencies
    5. Applicable skills
    6. Security constraints
    7. Project architecture
    8. Global protocol
    9. Historical context

  This ordering is the token economy foundation — AI reads the smallest
  sufficient context first (bax TokenEconomy in architecture/architecture.sdd
  -> Principles).


FailureRouting:
  Failure MUST be classified before routing:

    code issue      -> BE
    architecture    -> AR
    database        -> DB
    security        -> SC
    requirements    -> HUMAN
    infrastructure  -> DO

  AI MUST NOT blindly return to the previous stage. It MUST classify
  the failure type first, then route to the correct owner. This is
  controlled return, not chain loop (bax tasks/tasks.sdd -> FailureRouting).


HumanApproval:
  Three-level approval model:

    AUTO       — AI decides without human involvement
    REVIEW     — AI proposes, human reviews before execution
    BLOCK      — human decision required before any action

  Examples:
    Code formatting           AUTO
    Refactor                  AUTO / REVIEW
    Architecture change       REVIEW
    Security policy change    REVIEW
    Production destructive    BLOCK

  AI MUST respect these levels without exception.


ConflictResolution:
  Declared != Observed  -> DRIFT -> create task to reconcile
  Fact != Assumption    -> mark as ASSUMPTION, do not treat as fact
  Policy conflict       -> HUMAN_REVIEW -> escalate to human decision

State: +
  Note:
  This file is the constitution of the .sdd
  system. Every other file in .sdd/ is a specialization of these rules.
  The source proposed a `protocol/` directory at STEP 20/21/22 and it was
  declined each time because there was no coherent protocol layer yet —
   only isolated proposals. Research decisions now justify a
  single, authoritative protocol file: ROOT.sdd. INDEX.sdd is the entry
  point that routes to it. No additional protocol sub-files are created
  yet — the protocol layer is intentionally minimal at this phase
  (NoDuplication, skeleton + rule engine precedent).

  Token dictionary expanded with six new
  token categories: RelationTokens (o:/u:/c:/r:/w:/t:/s:/d:/doc:),
  ImpactTokens (I0-I5), RiskTokens (R0-R4), LevelTokens (L0-L5),
  EvidenceTokens (E:test/benchmark/incident/metric/audit/doc/pentest),
  TruthTokens (F/A/O/D/E). No separate protocol sub-files created —
  ROOT.sdd remains the single authoritative token dictionary (NoDuplication).

